  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\n**Independent verification. No new \u03ba, and no change to any construction,\ncertificate, selected record or pinned file.** It adds one registered package,\n[`research/two-connector-eight-transfer-census-seed58/`](research/two-connector-eight-transfer-census-seed58/README.md).\n\n**Priority belongs to #1615.** The six-transfer exclusion and the sharp\neight-transfer threshold for the free-port two-connector family are\n**#1615 (Hanson Sun / bbMisk)**. That family is the one from #1438/#1498: five\nsources, every B and A called exactly twice, and paid one-way transfers\n`C: z0 += z1`. The eight-transfer word is **Discussion #1412 (Cinna Davis)**.\nThis package was built concurrently without reading #1615's code. It agrees\nwith every #1615 number it overlaps.\n\n**What it adds:**\n\n1. **Re-certification in the generic CI hook.** #1615's registered hook is\n   `--witness-only`, and its exclusion needs a native C++ workflow.\n   - Here a gap-reduction lemma lets a pure-Python census certify the\n     exhaustive `t \u2264 7` exclusion and the exact `t = 8` count. On 10 cores it\n     takes about 20 s and 50 s, inside the generic Python 3.12 package hook.\n   - The lemma: within one gap `z1` is fixed. So three transfers act like one\n     and four like two, with coverage and restoration parities preserved.\n   - Two further code-disjoint engines cross-check it: a full-placement C census\n     and a numpy census with per-occurrence ports.\n2. **All eight-transfer solutions.**\n   - Exactly **2,502** canonical schedules are universal: **2,376 with one\n     source on `z1`** and **126 with all five on `z1`**. The second class is\n     Discussion #1412's restricted alphabet, up to renaming.\n   - No schedule with any other port split is universal at eight transfers.\n     The best coverages are 31, 31, 27 and 24 of 32.\n   - So free ports enlarge the solution set but save no transfer.\n   - All 2,502 replay as complete 80-call programs on dense random F2 maps with\n     arbitrary dirt.\n   - #1615's own census tool would give these counts with a small change. They\n     are recorded here with replay evidence.\n3. **#1498's four-transfer split bounds.** The r = 2 bound of 25 is **not\n   attained**; the exact maximum is 21. The other bounds are exact, as #1498\n   itself allowed (\"need not be sharp\").\n\n**Scope.** The incidence-minimal 72-call family only. It is a finite census\nplus a short reduction lemma. There is no supplier claim. The cost condition is\n#1498's `t\u00b7\u03c3` tariff at `t = 8`, as in #1615.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Base and track:** base `0b1cccf8`; catalogue priority revision 3 leaves the\n  card unchanged. Seed 58 \u2192 recursive interfaces, card\n  `connector-alphabet-change`, scope **refined** to independent verification\n  and classification.\n- **Overlap:**\n  - **#1615 (head `96916897`):** the threshold theorem. Its files contain no\n    eight-transfer count, no split classification, no `t = 4` split maxima and\n    no stdlib exclusion; I checked every file.\n  - **#1498 (`b7baacc8`):** the four-transfer obstruction and the question.\n  - **#1438 (merged, read in-repo):** the interface and full-placement censuses.\n  - No other overlapping work since #1615 was found among PRs or Discussions.\n\n## Validation\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/research_package.py --package research/two-connector-eight-transfer-census-seed58 --check-only\npython3.12 scripts/research_package.py --package research/two-connector-eight-transfer-census-seed58\n```\n\n**Registered parts** (standard library, parallel):\n\n- **`controls`:**\n  - #943's in-repo 105- and 945-word censuses, bin for bin;\n  - #1438's in-repo full-placement `t = 2` censuses for 1 to 5 sources: every\n    restored count and histogram;\n  - complete per-split coverage histograms pinned at `t = 0` and `t = 4`, with\n    no universal schedule;\n  - no restored schedule for odd `t`.\n- **`six`:** an exhaustive `t = 6` census over all 945 words and 32 splits.\n  None is universal, and the per-split maxima equal #1615's\n  (31, 31, 28, 22, 23, 31). The full histogram is pinned.\n- **`eight`:**\n  - exactly 2,502 universal schedules, with a pinned list digest `ba04f277\u2026`\n    and split counts;\n  - each replays as an 80-call program (62 A, 10 B, 8 C);\n  - Discussion #1412's pilot is included;\n  - on three witnesses, deleting any C or A, or reading on the wrong port, fails.\n\n**Offline receipts.**\n\n- **`independent/census.c`** (every placement, no reduction) agrees exactly:\n\n  | `t` | Candidates | Restored | Universal |\n  | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n  | 4 | 30,270,240 | 10,402,560 | 0 |\n  | 6 | 242,161,920 | 74,692,800 | 0 |\n  | 8 | 1,323,241,920 | 384,199,200 | 2,502 |\n\n  The `t = 6` totals reproduce #1615.\n- **`independent/census_numpy.py`** (per-occurrence ports) gives 74,692,800\n  legal schedules and 0 universal at `t = 6`.\n\n**Pre-PR review.** A three-lens review covered the mathematics, verifier\nsoundness and the claims.\n\n- The math reviewer's own fresh brute force reproduced every count:\n  - full-vector, per-occurrence ports for n = 1\u20133;\n  - a full-placement n = 5 C census, including all 2,502 at `t = 8`, each with\n    maximum gap 2;\n  - numeric readout equivalence over 92,772 checks.\n- Fixes from the review are included:\n  - complete-histogram pins, which catch an undercounting engine that the\n    maxima alone miss;\n  - live \"none universal\" checks and a `t = 0` all-split check;\n  - mutation controls on three witnesses;\n  - the merged #1438 read in-repo;\n  - wording.\n\nTested on Python 3.12.15 and 3.14.8 (macOS, 10 cores). On a 4-core runner each\npart fits well within the 600 s per-command limit.\n\n## Credit\n\n- **Hanson Sun (bbMisk), #1615:** the exclusion and the threshold, with\n  priority.\n- **Gareth Davidson (bitplane), #1498:** the obstruction, source-port lemma,\n  tariff and question.\n- **woklerx, #1438:** the interface and censuses.\n- **Cinna Davis (1337isnot1337), Discussion #1412:** the eight-transfer word.\n- **Ross Richardson, #943:** the universal connector and 945-word census.\n\nAI disclosure: prepared by Sagar Thakrar with substantial Anthropic Claude\nassistance, including explorer, judge and reviewer subagents. Two of those\nsubagents wrote the independent engines. New work is Apache-2.0.\n\n## Open next questions\n\n1. **Is there a short structural proof of the six-transfer exclusion that also\n   explains why only r = 1 and r = 5 reach eight?**\n   - *First step:* classify the 2,502 witnesses by their transfer phases.\n   - *Blocker:* none.\n2. **Can extra A/B incidences lower the threshold?** This is #1615's question 1.\n   - *First step:* extend `engine.py` to 73 calls with full dirt vectors.\n   - *Blocker:* the paired-readout reduction no longer applies.\n",
